Day 11: Tensor

Tensor is a data structure.

All the libraries we use today like scikit learn and TensorFlow use tensor as a data structure.
In any machine learning we solve today, tensor is the most important thing.
It helps in machine learning as well as deep learning.

Tensor is very important. One famous library is even named after it: TensorFlow.

Tensor is the container for numbers. Sometimes we use text too, but 99% of the time it is numbers.

If 0 dimension, it is a scalar.
If 1 dimension, it is a vector.
If 2 dimensions, it is a matrix.
If 3 or 4 dimensions, it is a tensor.

0D tensor: also called scalar. It has no dimension.

Using Google Colab:

import numpy as np

a = np.array(4)

print(a)

It will reply array(4)

To find dimension:
print(a.ndim)   # ndim = number of dimensions

That is why it is a 0D tensor.

1D tensor / vector (or 1D array):

[1, 2, 3]
Now ndim will be 1.

arr = np.array([1, 2, 3])
arr gives array([1, 2, 3])
Here it is a 1D tensor but a 3 element vector.
arr.ndim = 1

In 1D tensor it is a vector.

2D tensor / matrix:
Has 2 dimensions and 2 axes.
Number of dimensions = number of axes.

[[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]]

Matrices are nothing but a collection of vectors.
It has two axes: rows and columns.

3D tensor or more:
It is a combination of matrices. It has 3 axes.
4D tensor is a collection of 3D tensors.
5D tensor is a combination of 4D tensors.

In machine learning we usually go up to 5D tensor from 1D.

Rank, axes, and shape:
Rank = number of dimensions = number of axes

Shape: for example in a 2x3 matrix, shape is (2, 3).
Size of items = 2 x 3 = 6

For scalar, size is always 1.
For vector (1, 2, 3), shape = 3

Example of 1D tensor:

For 10k students, build a classification to find if placement will happen or not.

Kathmandu is 0 and Pokhara is 1.
Placement yes = 1 and no = 0.

cgpa | iq | state | placement
8.1 | 91 | 0 | 1
7.2 | 100 | 1 | 0

[8.1, 91, 0] is a 1D tensor. We have 3 values: cgpa, iq, and state. It is a 3 element vector but still a 1D tensor.
If there are 50 values, it is a 50 element vector but still 1D tensor.

Example of 2D tensor:

[cgpa, iq, location]
[[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]]

It is all the data in a table. 1D is one row only. This is 2D tensor.

3D tensor:

Best example is NLP.
Text like:
Hi Bishal
Hi ranjit
Hi tester

Words: hi | Bishal | ranjit | tester

hi      = [1, 0, 0, 0]   1D tensor
Bishal  = [0, 1, 0, 0]   1D tensor
ranjit  = [0, 0, 1, 0]   1D tensor
tester  = [0, 0, 0, 1]   1D tensor

For "Hi Bishal":
[[1, 0, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0, 0]]   2D tensor

For "Hi ranjit":
[[1, 0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 1, 0]]   2D tensor

For "Hi tester":
[[1, 0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0, 1]]   2D tensor

Combining all sentences gives a 3D tensor.

Another best example of 3D tensor is time series data.
Share market data taken over multiple time points.
For one stock we take highest price and lowest price.

For 1 year we get about 365 trading days x 2 values.
Shape (365, 2) is 2D tensor.

What if it is for 10 years?
Shape becomes (10, 365, 2). Here 365 is the time axis. This is 3D tensor.

4D tensor:

Best example is image data.
An image is a collection of pixels. A pixel is a numerical value.

For a color image we use 3 channels: red, green, and blue.
Example shape: (3, 1200, 800)

What if we have multiple images?
50 images: (50, 3, 1200, 800). This is 4D tensor.

5D tensor:

Best example is video.

Suppose we have a 60 second video:
60 seconds
30 fps
480p resolution
3 color channels

A collection of many videos becomes a 5D tensor.
